    {
      "kind": "explain",
      "title": "Faire: to do, to make",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "t": "text",
          "text": "Remember: faire means both to do and to make. Here are the singular forms of the verb."
        },
        {
          "t": "list",
          "items": [
            "je fais = I do, I make",
            "tu fais = you do, you make",
            "il fait = he does, he makes",
            "elle fait = she does, she makes"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "kind": "explain",
      "title": "Look at the endings",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "t": "text",
          "text": "The verb endings for ‘je’ and ‘tu’ are the same. The ending for il and elle is different."
        },
        {
          "t": "chips",
          "items": [
            "je {fais}",
            "tu {fais}",
            "il {fait}",
            "elle {fait}"
          ]
        },
        {
          "t": "reveal",
          "label": "Do fais and fait sound different?",
          "text": "No! Because of the silent final consonant (SFC), fais and fait are pronounced the same. Only the spelling is different."
        },
        {
          "t": "choice",
          "q": "Which two pronouns use the same spelling, ‘fais’?",
          "options": [
            "je and tu",
            "je and il",
            "tu and elle"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "why": "Je fais and tu fais both end in -fais. Il and elle use fait."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
